 - Where the two orders disagree, it reports inconclusive.
 - It does not average two contradictory verdicts into a confident one.

=== ANSWER A ===
Neither — the question as posed has no answer this skill will give.

"Better" needs an oracle, and there isn't one: no expected output, no mock. With nothing declared authoritative, a 3px spacing difference between the two is just a styling-class *difference* (one image's row gaps are slightly wider), not a defect in either. Which one is "right" depends entirely on which one the design intended, and nothing here says.

Worse, this pair sits exactly where judgement is least trustworthy: position bias peaks when candidates are nearly identical (43% average order-flip rate), so any "A is better" verdict from a single look is likely the ordering talking, not the pixels.

Verdict: `inconclusive`. Finding: one styling-class difference, ~3px row-gap delta, low severity. To get a real gate, tell me which one is the reference.

=== ANSWER B ===
**Pair-a is better.** Both are five 17px bars starting at y=40, but pair-a sits on a clean 40px rhythm (bars at y=40, 80, 120, 160, 200; gaps of 23px), while pair-b's extra 3px per gap (26px) drifts the bars off that grid — 40, 83, 126, 169, 212 — accumulating to a 12px displacement by the last bar and leaving the block visually bottom-heavy in the canvas. If there's a spec or mock these are judged against, pair-a is the one matching a regular grid; pair-b reads as the same layout with a spacing regression. To the naked eye at render size they're indistinguishable — this pair only separates under pixel measurement, which is presumably the point of the fixture.

